ready run once with status `ok`. I haven't seen it fire yet.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The `kibana_sample_data_logs` index has about 14,000 web requests, timed by the `timestamp` field, running from 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03. Some of those dates are in the future. Each request records:\n- **Who sent it:** the client IP address, user agent, operating system and source/destination country.\n- **What was asked for:** the URL, file extension, host and referrer.\n- **What came back:** the response code and size in bytes.\n\nThere are only three response codes: 12,832 are `200`, 801 are `404` and 441 are `503`. So the only server errors are `503`s.\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-8a91b123 Web logs overview\"** has 7 panels and opens on the last 7 days. It's at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-8a91b123-web-logs-overview-50bfd1ad5493\n- Three single numbers: total requests, unique visitors (distinct client IPs) and total bytes served.\n- Requests over time, split by response code.\n- A pie chart of response codes.\n- A table of the top 10 URLs, with request count and average bytes.\n- A bar chart of the top 10 source countries.\n\n**3. Alert rule \"EFF-8a91b123 5xx spike\"** (id `d5837dd0-2af8-4884-9dee-beff721c13d8`) checks every minute. It counts requests whose response code starts with \"5\" over the last 5 minutes and fires when the count is more than 10. The response code is stored as text, not a number, which is why the rule matches on the leading \"5\" rather than a 500–599 range.\n\nThe rule has no action attached, so when it fires it only shows up in Kibana's alerts list. Nobody gets notified until you add a connector such as Slack or email.","ttft_ms":1846,"type":"result","duration_ms":29275,"uuid":"765b3be2-316a-497d-ad85-531db7909b6e","ttft_stream_ms":1597,"time_to_request_ms":95,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":22,"system_prompt":9,"resume_other":1,"process_user_input":3,"input_attachments":12,"input_hooks":4,"tool_pool_refresh":4,"init_message":